{
  "teamId": 110,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Baltimore Orioles entered the season with genuine optimism, buoyed by a talented core and high expectations after recent playoff runs. April delivered flashes of brilliance — Pete Alonso announced himself with a towering early home run, Jeremiah Jackson erupted with a go-ahead three-run shot, and Adley Rutschman's first homer of the year in late April felt like a promise. But for every spark, there was a bucket of cold water: a grand slam surrendered here, a walk-off defensive miscue there. The Orioles were a team caught between their potential and their inconsistency, never quite able to string enough together.\n\nMay and early June became a story of individual heroism masking collective fragility. Colton Cowser's stunning three-run blast on May 24 was the season's most electrifying moment to that point, a 0.789 WPA swing that briefly made Baltimore feel like genuine contenders. But the bullpen, led by the embattled Andrew Kittredge and an overmatched Anthony Nunez, kept handing victories back. A catastrophic late-June collapse against the Angels — three crushing singles in a single game surrendering multiple runs — exposed just how thin the Orioles' pitching depth truly was.\n\nThe dog days of summer were defined by Jeremiah Jackson and Coby Mayo carrying Baltimore on their backs while the roster around them sputtered. Jackson's clutch double in July and Mayo's inside-the-park home run in September were genuine highlights in an otherwise grinding middle stretch. But the bullpen continued to bleed — Kittredge alone appeared in four of the season's most damaging moments — and September brought a pair of brutal late losses that effectively closed the door on playoff hopes.\n\nIn the end, the 2026 Orioles finished 79-82, a mirror of their fractured identity: enough power to excite, not enough pitching consistency to win. Pete Alonso's 40th home run on the season's final weekend felt bittersweet — a superstar flourish on a team that never found its footing. The pieces are there for a bounce-back, but this was a year when the sum fell well short of its parts.",
